  "query_preview": "..\nIssued 1101 PM CDT Mon Jul 27 2026\n\nThursday through Monday:\n\nHigh pressure will begin to push east on Thursday, allowing for\nsouthwesterly sfc return flow over Wisconsin. While the lingering\nhigh should keep us dry through the day, surface dew points will\nmodestly moisten into the mid to upper 60s, with afternoon highs\nagain returning to the upper 80s. \n\nThursday night into Friday, low to mid level warm advection is \nexpected ahead of an approaching elongated trough over the western\nCorn Belt. Showers and storms are expected on the leading edge of\nthis warm advection, but deterministic runs of the ECMWF and GFS \ndiffer in the track of the trough and storms for the day on \nFriday. The GFS favors a more northerly track that would keep rain\naround for most of the day on Friday, while the ECMWF guidance \nfavors a more southerly track that would bring us a glancing blow \nof rain in the morning, with drier conditions in the afternoon. \nNBM PoPs somewhat favor the GFS solution at this time bringing \nlikely PoPs (~60%) into our area Friday afternoon into Friday \nevening. Chance PoPs (30 to 45%) then linger into Saturday \nmorning as the trough slowly pulls away to the east and south.\n\nA sfc ridge will then build in behind the exiting trough,\npromoting dry conditions for Sunday and Monday. Temperatures\nFriday through Sunday will remain seasonable with highs in the low\n80s. By the end of the extended period, it looks like another\nridge from the plains leans east into Wisconsin, bringing warmer\ntemperatures in the upper 80s to 90 on Tuesday.\n\nCMiller",
